### 📚 Description

this stems from of a gap spotted when migrating a few modules to `nuxt/server` implementations. for example, `@nuxtjs/color-mode` needs to add some html to the initial render (see https://github.com/nuxt-modules/color-mode/pull/478 and comments).

as there is no server 'instance' when not using nitro, this was impossible.

so this PR adds renderer plugins, which allow attaching module code to the renderer rather than to a particular server:

```ts
// module.ts
addRendererPlugin(resolve('./runtime/server/renderer'))

// runtime/server/renderer.ts
import type { RendererPlugin } from 'nuxt/server'

export default (({ hooks }) => {
  hooks.hook('render:html', (html) => {
    html.head.push(`<script>${script}</script>`)
  })
}) satisfies RendererPlugin
```

each renderer calls the plugin once, with hooks of its own, which run before any registered on the server runtime (e.g. by nitro plugins). the plugin can import from `nuxt/server` (`useRuntimeConfig`, `getRouteRules`, etc.), and response headers can be set on `event.res.headers` from `render:html`.

on nitro the renderer runs them itself; on `@nuxt/vite-server` they're bundled into whatever runtime renders (node, workers). we do run the hooks when rendering a static SPA, at build time as a prerendered route.

on an older Nuxt without renderer plugins, `addRendererPlugin` registers the same file as a nitro plugin (nitro calls it with its app, whose `hooks` match), or a `nitro2`/`nitro3` variant if one is pass
